Docker architecture (Engine, CLI, Registry)
  Docker cli     ->(Rest API call) Docker daemon manages containers  -> (pull <-> pull) -> Docker hub registery,ecr
                                    |
                                    containers running process

  The three pieces
1. Docker CLI (client)

The docker command you type — docker run, docker build, docker ps, etc. It's just a thin client:
it doesn't do any actual container work itself. Every command gets translated into a REST API call sent to the daemon.

2. Docker Engine (daemon — dockerd)

This is where the real work happens — a persistent background process that:

Builds images (docker build)
Creates, starts, stops containers
Manages networking (creates the docker0 bridge, sets up iptables NAT rules — ties back to our earlier port-exposure discussion)
Manages volumes/storage
Pulls/pushes images to registries on your behalf

Internally, dockerd doesn't directly run containers itself either — it delegates to containerd (a lower-level container runtime), which in turn uses runc to actually create the isolated process (namespaces + cgroups, from our container-vs-VM discussion) at the OS level.

docker CLI → dockerd → containerd → runc → actual container process

  3. Registry

A registry stores and distributes images — the packaged, versioned filesystem + config that containers are created from.

bash
docker pull nginx:latest        # pulls from Docker Hub (default public registry)
docker pull myregistry.com/myapp:v2   # pulls from a private/custom registry
docker push myregistry.com/myapp:v2   # pushes your built image up

Common registries:

Registry	Type
Docker Hub	Public, default
AWS ECR	Private, cloud-native
Google Artifact Registry	Private, cloud-native
Azure Container Registry	Private, cloud-native
Harbor	Self-hosted, open-source

Images are identified by repository:tag (e.g., nginx:1.25), and are made of layers — each Dockerfile instruction (RUN, COPY, etc.) creates a new layer,
cached and reused across builds/pulls for efficiency.

# Images
docker pull nginx:latest
docker build -t myapp:v1 .
docker images
docker rmi myapp:v1
docker system prune -a

# Containers
docker run -d --name myweb -p 8080:80 -v ./data:/usr/share/nginx/html nginx
docker ps -a
docker logs -f <container>
docker exec -it <container> bash
docker stop <container> && docker rm <container>
docker port <container>            # check port mapping

# Compose
docker compose up -d
docker compose down
docker compose logs -f

The writable container layer

When you docker run an image, Docker adds one more layer on top — the only writable one. Everything that happens while the container runs 
(temp files, logs, downloaded data) goes there.

This layer is ephemeral — deleted when the container is removed (docker rm), unless the data lives in a mounted volume.
It's why multiple containers from the same image never interfere with each other — each gets its own separate writable layer on top of the same shared, 
read-only image layers underneath.

CMD — default command when the container starts
dockerfile
CMD ["node", "server.js"]

Only one CMD per Dockerfile (last one wins if multiple are specified). Can be overridden at runtime:

bash
docker run myapp node other-script.js    # overrides the CMD
ENTRYPOINT — the fixed command that always runs
dockerfile
ENTRYPOINT ["python3", "app.py"]

Unlike CMD, this is not easily overridden — docker run myapp --debug appends --debug as an argument to the entrypoint, rather than replacing it.

ENTRYPOINT + CMD combo (very common pattern — fixed command, overridable default args):

dockerfile
ENTRYPOINT ["python3", "app.py"]
CMD ["--port", "8080"]
bash
docker run myapp                    # runs: python3 app.py --port 8080
docker run myapp --port 9000        # runs: python3 app.py --port 9000 (overrides CMD's args

VOLUME — declare a mount point
dockerfile
VOLUME /data

Tells Docker this path should be treated as persistent/externally managed storage, separate from the container's writable layer.

HEALTHCHECK — define how Docker checks if the container is healthy
dockerfile
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s CMD curl -f http://localhost:8080/health || exit 1

Shows up in docker ps as (healthy) / (unhealthy) — useful for orchestrators (Kubernetes, Swarm, load balancers) deciding whether to route traffic to this container.                                                                        

The four core network drivers
1. bridge — the default
bash
docker run -d --name web nginx
# implicitly uses the default "bridge" network

Docker creates a virtual bridge (docker0 on Linux) — a private internal subnet (e.g., 172.17.0.0/16). Each container gets its own IP on that subnet, and traffic to/from the outside world goes through NAT (exactly the NAT concept from earlier — the container's private IP gets translated to the host's IP for outbound traffic).

bash
docker network inspect bridge

Important limitation of the default bridge network: containers can only reach each other by IP, not by name, unless you create a custom bridge network:

bash
docker network create mynet
docker run -d --name web --network mynet nginx
docker run -d --name api --network mynet myapi

Now web can reach api by hostname (http://api:3000) — Docker runs an embedded DNS server on custom networks that resolves container names automatically. This is the standard way multi-container apps talk to each other (and exactly what Docker Compose sets up for you automatically).

2. host — no isolation, shares the host's network stack directly
bash
docker run -d --network host nginx

The container skips the bridge/NAT layer entirely — it binds directly to the host's network interfaces. If nginx listens on port 80 inside the container, it's immediately on port 80 on the host — no -p mapping needed or even possible.

Trade-off: better performance (no NAT overhead), but zero network isolation — the container can bind to any port the host allows, and there's no port-mapping flexibility (can't run two containers both wanting port 80). Generally avoided unless you specifically need raw network performance or are doing low-level network tooling.

3. none — fully isolated, no networking at all
bash
docker run -d --network none myapp

The container gets a loopback interface only (127.0.0.1) — no external network access whatsoever. Useful for security-sensitive batch jobs that process data locally and need zero network exposure (defense in depth — even if the app were compromised, it has no network path out).

4. overlay — multi-host networking (Swarm/clustered setups)
bash
docker network create -d overlay myoverlay

Lets containers on different physical Docker hosts communicate as if they were on the same network — used in Docker Swarm clusters. Conceptually similar to what a Kubernetes CNI plugin does for pod-to-pod networking across nodes, just Docker's native version of it.

Port publishing vs Docker networking (tying back to earlier)

Networking modes control container-to-container and container-to-host communication. Publishing ports (-p 8080:80) is the separate mechanism that exposes a container port to the outside world, and only applies meaningfully to bridge networking (with host mode, there's nothing to "publish" — it's already on the host directly).

bash
docker run -d -p 8080:80 --network mynet nginx
Inspecting and debugging container networking
bash
docker network ls                          # list all networks
docker network inspect mynet               # see connected containers, subnet, gateway
docker inspect <container> | grep IPAddress # find a specific container's IP
docker exec -it <container> ping other-container   # test connectivity between containers (needs custom network for name resolution)
Practical example: a typical multi-container app
bash
docker network create appnet

docker run -d --name db --network appnet -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret postgres
docker run -d --name api --network appnet -e DB_HOST=db myapi
docker run -d --name web --network appnet -p 8080:80 -e API_HOST=api mynginx
api connects to db using hostname db — Docker's embedded DNS resolves it
web connects to api using hostname api — same mechanism
Only web publishes a port to the host (-p 8080:80) — db and api stay completely internal, unreachable from outside the Docker network entirely

This is effectively the same tiered isolation principle as the 3-tier security group design from earlier (LB → App → DB) — just implemented at the container-network level instead of the cloud-VPC level. The database container has no publish port at all, so it's simply unreachable from outside no matter what — a much stronger guarantee than a firewall rule that could be misconfigured.

In Docker Compose (this is handled automatically)
yaml
services:
  db:
    image: postgres
  api:
    image: myapi
    environment:
      - DB_HOST=db          # works automatically — compose creates a shared network
  web:
    image: mynginx
    ports:
      - "8080:80"

Compose automatically creates a custom bridge network for all services in the file, and every service can reach every other service by service name — no manual docker network create needed.

Docker Compose lets you define and run multi-container applications using a single YAML file, instead of stringing together long docker run commands manually. It ties together everything we've covered — networking, volumes, port mapping, environment variables — into one declarative config.

The core file: docker-compose.yml
yaml
version: "3.9"

services:
  web:
    image: nginx
    ports:
      - "8080:80"
    volumes:
      - ./html:/usr/share/nginx/html
    depends_on:
      - api
    networks:
      - appnet

  api:
    build: ./api
    environment:
      - DB_HOST=db
      - DB_PASSWORD=secret
    networks:
      - appnet

  db:
    image: postgres:16
    environment:
      - POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret
    volumes:
      - dbdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data
    networks:
      - appnet

volumes:
  dbdata:

networks:
  appnet:
Key sections explained
services

Each service is essentially one docker run command, expressed declaratively. web, api, and db here are each their own container.

image vs build
yaml
web:
  image: nginx          # pull a pre-built image from a registry

api:
  build: ./api           # build from a Dockerfile in ./api instead

You can mix both in the same file — some services pulled, others built locally.

ports
yaml
ports:
  - "8080:80"

Same host:container mapping as docker run -p — only needed for services that must be reached from outside the Compose network (typically just the front-facing service, like web here).

volumes
yaml
volumes:
  - ./html:/usr/share/nginx/html     # bind mount (relative path)
  - dbdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data  # named volume (declared in top-level `volumes:`)

Same volume vs bind mount distinction from earlier — just expressed in YAML instead of -v flags.

environment
yaml
environment:
  - DB_HOST=db
  - DB_PASSWORD=secret

Equivalent to -e flags on docker run. Can also load from a file:

yaml
env_file:
  - .env
depends_on
yaml
depends_on:
  - api

Controls startup order only — api starts before web. Important caveat: this does NOT wait for api to be actually ready (e.g., finished booting, listening on its port) — just that the container process has started. For true readiness, pair with a healthcheck:

yaml
api:
  healthcheck:
    test: ["CMD", "curl", "-f", "http://localhost:3000/health"]
    interval: 10s
    retries: 5

web:
  depends_on:
    api:
      condition: service_healthy
networks
yaml
networks:
  appnet:

Compose creates this custom bridge network automatically even if you don't declare it explicitly — every service in the file gets attached, and (as covered in the bridge/custom networks discussion) can reach every other service by service name thanks to Compose's automatic DNS resolution.

Core commands
bash
docker compose up                 # start everything, foreground (see all logs)
docker compose up -d               # start everything, detached
docker compose down                 # stop and remove containers, networks (keeps volumes)
docker compose down -v               # ALSO removes volumes — data loss risk, as covered earlier
docker compose ps                     # list services and their status
docker compose logs -f                 # follow logs across ALL services
docker compose logs -f api              # follow logs for just one service
docker compose exec api sh               # shell into a running service
docker compose build                      # rebuild images for services using `build:`
docker compose up -d --build                # rebuild + restart in one step
docker compose restart web                    # restart a single service
docker compose stop / start                     # stop/start without removing containers
Scaling a service
bash
docker compose up -d --scale api=3

Runs 3 instances of the api service — useful for quick local load-testing (in production, Kubernetes' Deployment replicas handle this more robustly, but Compose supports basic scaling too).

Multiple Compose files (environment overrides)
bash
docker-compose.yml           # base config
docker-compose.override.yml  # auto-merged in dev (Compose applies this automatically)
docker-compose.prod.yml      # explicit prod overrides
bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d

Common pattern: keep shared config in the base file, then override just what differs per environment (e.g., different environment values, no bind mounts in prod, different replica counts).

.env file — variable substitution
bash
# .env
DB_PASSWORD=supersecret
WEB_PORT=8080
yaml
services:
  web:
    ports:
      - "${WEB_PORT}:80"
  db:
    environment:
      - POSTGRES_PASSWORD=${DB_PASSWORD}

Compose automatically loads .env from the same directory — keeps secrets/config out of the committed YAML file (should still be .gitignore'd, same as any secret).
                                                                        
Docker's embedded DNS resolves db to whatever internal IP that container currently has — no hardcoded IPs, no manual lookup, and it keeps working even if the container restarts and gets a new IP.
                                                                                                                
version: "3.9"        # Compose file format version (mostly informational in modern Compose v2)

services:              # ← top-level: defines each container/app component
  web:                  # ← service name — becomes the hostname other services use to reach it
    image: nginx          # use a pre-built image (pulled from registry)
    build: ./api           # OR build from a Dockerfile in this directory (use one or the other, or both — build + custom tag)
    ports:
      - "8080:80"           # host_port:container_port — publishes to the HOST, makes it externally reachable
    volumes:
      - ./html:/usr/share/nginx/html   # bind mount: host_path:container_path
      - dbdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data # named volume: volume_name:container_path (must be declared below)
    environment:
      - DB_HOST=db            # env vars injected INTO the container at runtime
    env_file:
      - .env                   # load a whole file of env vars at once
    depends_on:
      - api                     # controls STARTUP ORDER only (not readiness, unless combined with healthcheck condition)
    networks:
      - appnet                   # attaches this service to a named network (for name-based DNS to other services)
    healthcheck:                  # defines how Compose/Docker checks if this container is "healthy"
      test: ["CMD", "curl", "-f", "http://localhost/health"]
      interval: 10s
      retries: 5
    restart: unless-stopped         # restart policy: no | always | on-failure | unless-stopped
    command: ["npm", "start"]         # OVERRIDES the image's default CMD
    entrypoint: ["/bin/sh", "-c"]       # OVERRIDES the image's default ENTRYPOINT

volumes:                # ← top-level: declares NAMED volumes used by any service above
  dbdata:                  # just declaring the name — Docker manages the actual storage

networks:                # ← top-level: declares custom networks used by any service above
  appnet:                   # Compose creates this network; services join via `networks:` under each service
                                                                                                                
 To directly answer the "running state" question
docker compose build → no containers need to be running; it's purely a build step, works even on a completely fresh machine with nothing started yet.
docker compose up → this is what actually creates and starts containers for services defined in the file — after up, yes, all listed services (unless you scoped it) will be in a running state.
You can build without ever running (build only), or run without ever explicitly building (up triggers a build automatically the first time if build: is set and no image exists yet)
                                                                                                                
