Core / Conceptual (expect these to be quick, but watch for depth)

Q: What's the difference between a Docker image and a container?
A: An image is a read-only, layered filesystem template built from a Dockerfile — it's immutable and doesn't run. A container is a running instance of that image with a writable layer on top, plus its own process namespace, network namespace, etc. Many containers can spin up from the same image.

Q: Explain Docker's layered filesystem and why layer caching matters.
A: Each Dockerfile instruction (RUN, COPY, ADD, etc.) creates a new layer, and layers are cached and reused if unchanged. Ordering matters for build speed — put things that change rarely (base image, system deps) early, and things that change often (app source code) late, so cache invalidation doesn't cascade unnecessarily and force a full rebuild.

Q: What's the difference between CMD and ENTRYPOINT?
A: ENTRYPOINT defines the fixed executable that always runs; CMD supplies default arguments that can be overridden at docker run. Using both together (ENTRYPOINT ["python", "app.py"] + CMD ["--port", "8080"]) is a common pattern to let users override just the args.

Practical / Production-level (this is where 5 YOE should shine)

Q: How do you reduce Docker image size in production?
A:

Use multi-stage builds — compile/build in one stage, copy only the final artifact into a slim runtime stage.
Use minimal base images (alpine, distroless) where compatible.
Combine RUN commands to reduce layers, and clean up package manager caches in the same layer they were created (not a later RUN, since that doesn't shrink earlier layers).
Use .dockerignore to avoid copying unnecessary files (.git, node_modules, test data) into the build context.

Q: What's the difference between COPY and ADD?
A: COPY is the straightforward, preferred choice — copies files/directories as-is. ADD has extra magic: it can fetch remote URLs and auto-extract tar archives. Best practice is to default to COPY unless you specifically need ADD's extraction/URL behavior, since the implicit behavior can cause surprises.

Q: How does container networking work — bridge vs host vs none vs overlay?
A:

bridge (default): containers get their own virtual network, isolated from host, with NAT for outbound and port mapping for inbound.
host: container shares the host's network namespace directly — no isolation, but no NAT overhead.
none: no networking at all.
overlay: spans multiple Docker hosts, used in Swarm/multi-host setups for cross-node container communication.

Q: How do you persist data in Docker, and what's the difference between volumes and bind mounts?
A:

Volumes: managed by Docker, live under /var/lib/docker/volumes, portable, easier to back up, work well across environments — the recommended default.
Bind mounts: map a specific host path into the container. Useful for local dev (live code reload) but tightly couples the container to host filesystem layout — avoid in production images meant to be portable.

Q: A container keeps restarting/crashing in production — how do you debug it?
A good answer walks through a sequence, not just one command:

docker logs <container> first, and docker logs --tail 100 -f if it's still running intermittently.
docker inspect <container> to check exit code, restart policy, resource limits (was it OOMKilled?).
docker events to correlate timing with host-level events.
If the app exits before logs are useful, override the entrypoint temporarily (docker run -it --entrypoint sh <image>) to poke around interactively.
Check resource constraints (docker stats) — many "random" crashes are OOM kills from too-tight memory limits.

Q: Explain the difference between docker-compose and Docker Swarm/Kubernetes — when would you pick which?
A: Compose is for defining and running multi-container apps on a single host — great for local dev/staging. Swarm and Kubernetes are for orchestration across multiple hosts — scaling, self-healing, rolling updates, service discovery. At 5 YOE you should be able to talk about why a team outgrows Compose (no multi-host scheduling, no built-in health-check-based rescheduling, no native load balancing across replicas).

Q: What are Docker health checks and why do they matter in orchestration?
A: HEALTHCHECK in a Dockerfile lets Docker (and orchestrators) know if a container is actually working, not just running. A process can be alive but deadlocked/unresponsive — health checks let Swarm/K8s automatically restart or stop routing traffic to unhealthy instances rather than relying on process-exit-code alone.

Q: How do you handle secrets (API keys, DB passwords) in Docker — and what should you never do?
A: Never bake secrets into the image via ENV or ARG in the Dockerfile — they persist in image layers/history and are visible via docker history or docker inspect. Use:

Docker secrets (Swarm) or Kubernetes Secrets for orchestrated environments.
Runtime environment injection (--env-file, or your orchestrator's secret store) rather than build-time.
External secret managers (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager) with the app fetching secrets at startup.

Q: What's the difference between docker stop and docker kill?
A: docker stop sends SIGTERM, waits a grace period (default 10s) for graceful shutdown, then sends SIGKILL if it hasn't exited. docker kill sends SIGKILL (or a specified signal) immediately — no grace period.

Scenario/System-design style (common at senior level)

Q: How would you set up a CI/CD pipeline for building and deploying Docker images?
A: Talk through: build stage (multi-stage Dockerfile, tag with commit SHA not just latest), push to a private registry, vulnerability scanning (Trivy/Snyk) as a gate, then deploy via orchestrator with rolling update strategy, and rollback plan if health checks fail post-deploy.

Q: How do you limit CPU/memory for a container, and what happens if it exceeds the limit?
A: --memory and --cpus flags (or deploy.resources in Compose/K8s). Exceeding memory triggers an OOM kill of the container's main process. Exceeding CPU limit doesn't kill it — it just gets throttled by the kernel's CFS scheduler.

Q: Your image builds fine locally but fails in CI — what do you check?
A: Base image tag drift (latest vs pinned version), build context differences (.dockerignore mismatches), architecture mismatch (arm64 laptop vs amd64 CI runner), and cached layers from a stale local build masking a real issue that only shows on a clean CI build.                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
What is Cloud Computing?

Delivery of computing resources (servers, storage, databases, networking, software) over the internet ("the cloud"), on-demand, with pay-as-you-go pricing — instead of buying and maintaining physical hardware yourself.

Core Characteristics (NIST's definition, still the standard reference)
On-demand self-service: provision resources yourself, no human intervention from the provider needed.
Broad network access: available over the internet, from any device.
Resource pooling: provider's physical infrastructure serves many customers ("multi-tenancy"), with resources dynamically assigned.
Rapid elasticity: scale up or down quickly based on demand.
Measured service: you pay for what you use, usage is metered/tracked.
Service Models (the classic "as-a-Service" stack)

IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service)
Raw compute, storage, networking — you manage the OS, runtime, and app.
Examples: AWS EC2, Azure VMs, Google Compute Engine.

PaaS (Platform as a Service)
Provider manages OS/runtime; you just deploy code.
Examples: Heroku, AWS Elastic Beanstalk, Google App Engine.

SaaS (Software as a Service)
Fully managed application, you just use it.
Examples: Gmail, Salesforce, Dropbox.

A common way to remember the tradeoff: as you move IaaS → PaaS → SaaS, you give up control but gain convenience — the provider manages more of the stack for you.

There's also FaaS (Function as a Service / serverless) — you just write functions (e.g. AWS Lambda), and the provider handles everything including scaling individual function invocations, with no persistent server to manage at all.

Deployment Models
Public cloud: shared infrastructure, managed by a third-party provider (AWS, Azure, GCP). Cheapest, most common.
Private cloud: dedicated infrastructure for a single organization, either on-prem or hosted. More control/compliance, more cost.
Hybrid cloud: mix of public + private, often for compliance reasons (sensitive data stays private, everything else goes public) or to burst extra capacity into public cloud during peak load.
Multi-cloud: using more than one public cloud provider, often to avoid vendor lock-in or leverage best-of-breed services.
Key Concepts You'll Run Into Constantly

Elasticity vs Scalability

Scalability: system's capacity to handle growth (can it scale?).
Elasticity: the automatic, dynamic adjustment of resources in response to real-time load (does it scale itself up/down as needed?).

Availability Zones & Regions

Region: a geographic area (e.g. us-east-1) containing multiple data centers.
Availability Zone (AZ): an isolated data center within a region, with independent power/cooling/networking, so a failure in one AZ doesn't take down the whole region.
Deploying across multiple AZs is the standard way to get high availability.

Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling

Vertical: bigger machine (more CPU/RAM on the same instance). Has a ceiling.
Horizontal: more machines running in parallel, load-balanced. This is what "cloud-native" scaling usually means.

Load Balancing
Distributes incoming traffic across multiple instances so no single server gets overwhelmed, and so a failed instance doesn't take the whole app down.

CapEx vs OpEx
Traditional infrastructure = CapEx (big upfront capital expense for hardware). Cloud = OpEx (ongoing operational expense, pay-as-you-go) — one of the main business reasons companies moved to cloud.

Shared Responsibility Model
Security is split between provider and customer:

Provider secures the cloud (physical data centers, host infrastructure, hypervisor).
Customer secures what's in the cloud (data, access management, OS patching depending on service model, app-level security).
This split shifts depending on IaaS vs PaaS vs SaaS — the more managed the service, the more the provider takes on.
