What is EC2?

Elastic Compute Cloud — AWS's IaaS offering for resizable virtual servers ("instances") in the cloud. You rent compute capacity instead of buying physical servers, and can scale up/down on demand.

Core Building Blocks

1. AMI (Amazon Machine Image)
The template used to launch an instance — includes OS, pre-installed software, and configuration. You can use:

AWS-provided AMIs (Amazon Linux, Ubuntu, Windows Server, etc.)
Marketplace AMIs (pre-configured by third parties)
Custom AMIs you create from your own configured instance (useful for consistent, repeatable deployments)

2. Instance Types
Named by family + generation + size, e.g. t3.medium, m5.large, c6g.xlarge. Families are optimized for different workloads:

Family	Optimized for	Example use case
T (t2, t3, t4g)	Burstable, general purpose, cheap	Dev/test, low-traffic web apps
M (m5, m6i)	Balanced compute/memory/network	General purpose production apps
C (c5, c6g)	Compute-optimized	High-CPU workloads, batch processing
R (r5, r6g)	Memory-optimized	In-memory caches, large databases
I (i3)	Storage-optimized, high IOPS	NoSQL databases, data warehousing
G/P (g4, p3)	GPU	ML training, video rendering

3. Key Pairs
Public/private key pair used for SSH (Linux) or RDP password decryption (Windows) authentication into an instance. AWS stores the public key; you keep the private key (.pem file) — lose it and you generally can't recover SSH access to that instance without extra steps.

4. Security Groups
Virtual firewall attached to an instance, controlling inbound/outbound traffic.

Stateful: if inbound traffic is allowed, the corresponding outbound response is automatically allowed (and vice versa) — you don't need matching rules both ways.
Rules are allow-only — you can't write explicit "deny" rules (that's what Network ACLs are for, at the subnet level).
Default: all inbound traffic denied, all outbound allowed, until you add rules.

5. Elastic IP
A static, public IPv4 address you can attach/detach from instances. Without one, an instance's public IP changes every time it's stopped and started — an Elastic IP keeps it fixed.

6. EBS (Elastic Block Store)
Persistent block storage volumes attached to instances (like a virtual hard drive). Data persists independently of instance lifecycle (unlike instance store, below). Comes in different volume types (gp3 general purpose, io2 high-IOPS, st1/sc1 for throughput-optimized cold storage).

7. Instance Store
Temporary storage physically attached to the host — much faster than EBS, but data is lost when the instance stops or terminates (not on reboot). Used for caches, temp data, scratch space — never for anything that needs to persist.

Instance Lifecycle States
pending → running → (stopping → stopped) → (rebooting) → terminated

Key distinctions:

Stop vs Terminate: Stop preserves EBS-backed root volumes (you can restart later, billed only for storage); Terminate deletes the instance and (by default) its root EBS volume permanently.
Reboot is like an OS-level restart — doesn't change the underlying host or lose instance store data.
Stopping/starting an instance may put it on a different physical host — this is why Elastic IPs matter (public IP would otherwise change).
Pricing Models
Model	How it works	Best for
On-Demand	Pay per second/hour, no commitment	Unpredictable, short-term workloads
Reserved Instances	1 or 3-year commitment, significant discount (~30-70%)	Steady, predictable long-term workloads
Savings Plans	Commit to $/hour spend (flexible across instance types), discount similar to RIs	Flexible long-term commitment
Spot Instances	Bid on unused AWS capacity, up to ~90% discount, but can be reclaimed with short notice	Fault-tolerant, flexible workloads (batch jobs, CI/CD)
Dedicated Hosts	Physical server dedicated to you	Compliance/licensing requirements (e.g. BYOL)
Auto Scaling & Load Balancing (tightly coupled with EC2)
Auto Scaling Group (ASG): automatically adds/removes instances based on demand (CPU, custom CloudWatch metrics, or schedule), maintains a minimum/desired/maximum instance count.
Elastic Load Balancer (ELB): distributes traffic across multiple instances (often across AZs) — Application Load Balancer (Layer 7, HTTP/HTTPS) vs Network Load Balancer (Layer 4, TCP, ultra-low latency) are the two most common types.

Together, ASG + ELB is the standard pattern for a scalable, highly-available web tier.

Common Interview/Practical Questions

Q: What happens to data on instance store vs EBS when you stop an instance?
A: Instance store data is lost. EBS-backed root volumes and any attached EBS volumes persist.

Q: How do you SSH into an EC2 instance if you lost your private key?
A: You can't directly recover the original key, but you can: stop the instance, detach its root volume, attach it to another instance as a secondary volume, add a new public key to authorized_keys on that volume, then reattach it to the original instance and restart.

Q: Security Group vs NACL?
A: Security Group = instance-level, stateful, allow-only. NACL = subnet-level, stateless (need explicit inbound AND outbound rules), supports both allow and deny rules — used as a coarser, secondary layer of defense.

Q: How would you reduce EC2 costs for a variable workload?
A: Mix of Reserved/Savings Plans for baseline predictable load + Spot Instances for burst/batch capacity + Auto Scaling to avoid over-provisioning + right-sizing instance types based on actual CPU/memory utilization (via CloudWatch).                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
Quick Summary Table
	Security Group	NACL (Network ACL)
Level	Instance-level (attached to ENI/instance)	Subnet-level (attached to a subnet)
Statefulness	Stateful — return traffic automatically allowed	Stateless — must explicitly allow both inbound and outbound
Rule types	Allow only — no explicit deny	Allow and Deny both supported
Rule evaluation	All rules evaluated, most permissive wins	Rules processed in order by rule number, first match wins
Scope	Applies only to instances explicitly associated with it	Applies to all instances in the subnet automatically
Default behavior	Denies all inbound by default, allows all outbound	Default NACL allows all traffic; custom NACLs deny all until you add rules
Key Concepts Explained

Stateful vs Stateless — the most important distinction

Security Group (stateful): If you allow inbound traffic on port 443, the response traffic going back out is automatically allowed — you don't need a matching outbound rule.
NACL (stateless): You must write rules for both directions independently. Allow inbound on port 443 doesn't automatically allow the outbound response — you need a separate outbound rule (often on an ephemeral port range, since return traffic uses high random ports).

This trips people up constantly — it's the #1 reason NACL misconfigurations break connectivity that "should" work.

Allow-only vs Allow+Deny

Security groups can't block specific IPs — you can only add rules for what's allowed. To block something, you simply don't add a rule for it.
NACLs let you explicitly deny — useful for blocking a known-bad IP range at the subnet level without touching every security group.

Rule order matters (NACL only)

NACL rules have numbers (e.g., 100, 200, 300) and are evaluated in ascending order — first match wins, and evaluation stops there. Security groups have no such ordering; all rules are evaluated together and the most permissive outcome applies.

How They Work Together (Defense in Depth)

Traffic passing into an instance goes through both layers:

Internet → NACL (subnet boundary) → Security Group (instance boundary) → Instance

NACLs act as a coarser, subnet-wide filter (first line of defense, e.g., block a malicious IP range for the whole subnet), while Security Groups give fine-grained, per-instance control (e.g., only allow port 22 from your office IP to this specific bastion host).

Practical Rule of Thumb
Security Groups: your primary, day-to-day tool — use for almost everything (which ports, which sources, which instances).
NACLs: mostly left at default (allow all) unless you have a specific need to block something at the subnet level — e.g., blacklisting a known bad actor's IP range across an entire subnet regardless of security group config.
Common Interview Follow-up

Q: Why would you need both if Security Groups already control access?
A: Defense in depth — if a security group is misconfigured too permissively, a NACL can still block malicious traffic at the subnet boundary before it even reaches the instance level. It's a second, independent layer that doesn't rely on per-instance configuration being correct.                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
S3 Fundamentals

S3 (Simple Storage Service) is AWS's object storage service — not a filesystem, not a block device. You store and retrieve arbitrary files ("objects") via HTTP(S) API calls, not through a mounted drive.

Key characteristics:

Virtually unlimited storage, no capacity provisioning needed.
11 nines (99.999999999%) durability — achieved by automatically replicating objects across multiple AZs within a region.
Flat namespace (no real folders) — what looks like folders in the console is just / characters in object keys, simulated visually.
Accessed via REST API, AWS CLI, SDKs, or console.

Storage Classes (cost vs retrieval-speed/frequency tradeoff):

Class	Use case	Retrieval
S3 Standard	Frequently accessed data	Instant
S3 Intelligent-Tiering	Unknown/changing access patterns	Instant, auto-moves between tiers
S3 Standard-IA (Infrequent Access)	Backups, DR, less-frequent access	Instant, but retrieval fee
S3 One Zone-IA	Same as above but single AZ (cheaper, less durable)	Instant
S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval	Archive, rarely accessed, need fast retrieval	Instant
S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval	Archive	Minutes to hours
S3 Glacier Deep Archive	Long-term archive, compliance	Up to 12 hours

Lifecycle policies automate moving objects between classes (e.g., "move to Glacier after 90 days, delete after 365 days") — a common cost-optimization interview topic.

Buckets & Objects

Bucket = top-level container for objects. Bucket names are globally unique across all of AWS (not just your account) — a common early gotcha.

Object = the actual file, plus metadata, stored with a unique key (its "path" within the bucket). An object's full identity = bucket + key + version ID (if versioning is on).

Key facts:

Max object size: 5 TB (single PUT is capped at 5 GB — anything larger requires multipart upload).
Objects are immutable — you can't "edit" an object in place, only overwrite it entirely with a new PUT.
Versioning (optional, per-bucket): keeps every version of an object when overwritten/deleted, protecting against accidental deletion/overwrite. Once enabled, can be suspended but never fully turned back off.
Object keys with / create the illusion of folders in the console UI, but S3 has no real directory structure underneath.
Permissions & Policies

S3 access control has several overlapping layers — a very common source of confusion, and a common interview question ("why is my bucket still public/private despite X setting?").

1. IAM Policies (attached to users/roles)
Control what an IAM identity can do across AWS, including S3 actions — this is the modern, recommended primary mechanism.

2. Bucket Policies (attached to the bucket itself)
JSON resource-based policies defining who can access the bucket and how — including cross-account access, since bucket policies can grant access to principals outside your own account.

3. ACLs (Access Control Lists) — legacy, mostly discouraged now
Older, more limited permission mechanism at the bucket or object level. AWS recommends disabling ACLs entirely and using bucket policies/IAM instead for new setups.

4. Block Public Access settings (account and bucket level)
A separate override layer that can force-block public access even if a policy/ACL would otherwise allow it — added specifically to prevent accidental public bucket exposure, which has historically been a very common (and damaging) misconfiguration. Best practice: leave this on unless you have a specific, deliberate reason for public access (e.g., static website hosting).

Evaluation logic: AWS evaluates all applicable policies together — if Block Public Access is on, it overrides everything else regardless of what the bucket policy says. Otherwise, if any policy (IAM, bucket policy) explicitly denies, that wins; otherwise if any explicitly allows, access is granted.

Common interview Q: "My bucket policy allows public read, but the bucket still isn't accessible — why?"
A: Almost always Block Public Access is enabled at the bucket or account level, overriding the policy.

Encryption (relevant to permissions/compliance discussions):

SSE-S3: AWS manages the keys entirely.
SSE-KMS: keys managed via AWS KMS — adds audit trail (CloudTrail) and finer-grained key permission control.
SSE-C: you supply your own encryption key with each request.
Client-side encryption: encrypt before upload, AWS never sees plaintext.
EBS Volumes

EBS (Elastic Block Store) = persistent block storage, attached to a single EC2 instance (with limited exceptions like io2 Multi-Attach) — behaves like a virtual hard drive, unlike S3's object model.

Key facts:

AZ-locked — an EBS volume only attaches to instances in the same Availability Zone. To move it, you snapshot it and restore into a different AZ.
Persists independently of instance lifecycle (survives stop/start; deleted only if you choose to delete it or if "delete on termination" is set for the root volume).

Volume types:

Type	Best for	Notes
gp3	General purpose (default recommendation)	Balanced price/performance, decoupled IOPS/throughput scaling
gp2	General purpose (older)	Performance tied to volume size
io2 / io2 Block Express	High-performance, mission-critical DBs	Highest IOPS, most durable, supports Multi-Attach
st1	Throughput-optimized HDD	Big sequential workloads (data warehousing, log processing)
sc1	Cold HDD	Infrequently accessed, lowest cost

EBS Snapshots:

Point-in-time backups of a volume, stored in S3 (though not directly accessible as S3 objects).
Incremental — after the first full snapshot, subsequent snapshots only store changed blocks, saving cost/time.
Can be copied across regions/accounts, and used to create new volumes or AMIs.
Backup Concepts

Core principles (apply broadly, not just AWS-specific):

RPO (Recovery Point Objective): how much data loss is acceptable, measured in time (e.g., "we can afford to lose up to 1 hour of data" → need backups/replication at least hourly).
RTO (Recovery Time Objective): how long you can tolerate being down before restoring service.
These two numbers drive your entire backup/DR architecture decision — tighter RPO/RTO = more expensive, more complex solution.

AWS-specific backup mechanisms:

Service/Feature	What it backs up
EBS Snapshots	Block volumes (incremental, stored in S3 internally)
AMI	Full instance image (root volume + config) — used to relaunch identical instances
RDS Automated Backups	Daily snapshots + transaction logs, enabling point-in-time restore
RDS Manual Snapshots	On-demand, retained until you delete them (unlike automated backups, which expire)
S3 Versioning	Protects against accidental overwrite/delete of objects
S3 Cross-Region Replication	Automatic copy of objects to a bucket in another region (DR, compliance)
AWS Backup	Centralized service to manage backup policies across EBS, RDS, DynamoDB, EFS, etc. from one place

Common DR strategy tiers (from cheapest/slowest to most expensive/fastest recovery) — a classic interview framing:

Backup & Restore — cheapest, highest RTO (hours) — just have backups, restore when needed.
Pilot Light — minimal core infrastructure always running in a second region, scaled up during failover.
Warm Standby — a scaled-down but fully functional copy running in a second region, scaled up on failover.
Multi-site Active/Active — full production capacity running simultaneously in multiple regions — lowest RTO/RPO, highest cost.

Common interview Q: "Difference between an EBS snapshot and an AMI?"
A: A snapshot backs up a single volume's data. An AMI is a full instance template — includes the root volume snapshot plus launch configuration (instance type compatibility, block device mappings) needed to launch a new instance from scratch, not just restore a volume.

CloudTrail vs CloudWatch — a very common point of confusion:

CloudWatch: monitors performance and operational health — metrics, logs, alarms ("is CPU high? is the app throwing errors?").
CloudTrail: logs API calls and account activity for audit/security — "who launched this instance, when, from what IP?"

In-Place Deployment Strategy

How it works: CodeDeploy stops the app on each instance, deploys the new version, restarts it — instance by instance (or in batches), on the same existing instances. No new infrastructure is created.

Characteristics:

Downtime per instance during its update window (briefly unavailable while stopped/restarted).
Cheapest option — no duplicate infrastructure needed.
Rollback is harder: to roll back, you have to redeploy the previous version through the same in-place process — the old version isn't sitting there ready to receive traffic again instantly.
Works with EC2/on-premises deployments (not applicable to Lambda or ECS, which use different deployment models).

When to use: cost-sensitive environments, dev/test, or workloads that can tolerate brief per-instance blips (especially with a rolling batch approach so the whole fleet isn't down simultaneously).

Blue-Green Deployment Strategy

How it works: Instead of updating existing instances, you provision a completely separate, parallel environment ("green") running the new version, alongside the currently live one ("blue"). Once green passes health checks, traffic is switched over — either all at once or gradually — from blue to green.

Characteristics:

Zero downtime — blue keeps serving traffic until green is verified healthy.
Instant, safe rollback — if something's wrong post-cutover, just switch traffic back to blue, which is still fully intact and running.
Costs more — you're running double the infrastructure during the transition window.
Traffic-shifting can be all-at-once, linear (shift a fixed % every X minutes), or canary (shift a small % first, wait, then shift the rest) — CodeDeploy supports configuring this directly.

Rolling Deployment Strategy

How it works: Instances/tasks are updated in batches, not all at once. A batch of the old version is taken out of rotation, updated to the new version, health-checked, and put back into rotation — then the next batch, until the whole fleet is updated.

Batch 1 (25%) → update → health check → back in rotation
Batch 2 (25%) → update → health check → back in rotation
Batch 3 (25%) → update → health check → back in rotation
Batch 4 (25%) → update → health check → back in rotation

Key characteristics:

No new infrastructure created — same instances get updated in place, just in waves rather than all simultaneously (distinguishing it from blue/green, which spins up a whole parallel environment).
Capacity dips during rollout unless you use "rolling with additional batch," which launches extra temporary capacity first so total capacity never drops below 100%.
Rollback is slower than blue/green — you have to roll the batches backward through the same process; there's no "old environment sitting idle" to just switch back to.
Both old and new versions run simultaneously during the rollout window — this matters for the same backward-compatibility reason as blue/green (schema/API compatibility across versions).

Canary Deployment Strategy

How it works: Deploy the new version to a small subset of traffic/instances first (the "canary" — named after canaries in coal mines used to detect danger early), monitor it closely, and only if it looks healthy do you progressively shift more traffic to it, eventually reaching 100%.

Step 1: 10% traffic → new version, 90% → old version
        [monitor error rates, latency, etc.]
Step 2: 50% traffic → new version
        [monitor again]
Step 3: 100% traffic → new version

Key characteristics:

Smallest blast radius if something's wrong — only a small fraction of users are affected before you catch the problem and can halt/rollback.
Requires real traffic-shifting capability at the load balancer/routing layer (weighted target groups, weighted Lambda aliases, weighted Route 53 records) — not just instance-level batching like rolling.
Usually paired with automated monitoring + automatic rollback — canary's whole value proposition depends on catching problems during the small-percentage window before wider rollout, so it's commonly wired to CloudWatch alarms that auto-abort if error rate/latency spikes.

A NAT Gateway (Network Address Translation Gateway) is a managed service that lets resources in a private subnet (no public IP) initiate outbound connections to the internet — without allowing anything from the internet to initiate a connection back in.

Why it exists

In your VPC, you want app servers, databases, etc. in private subnets for security — they shouldn't be directly reachable from the internet. But those servers often still need outbound access:
That's the exact gap NAT Gateway fills: outbound yes, inbound no.

Pulling OS/package updates
Calling external APIs (Stripe, SendGrid, etc.)
Downloading container images
Sending logs/metrics to an external SaaS

Example:

ENTRYPOINT ["java"]
CMD ["-jar", "app.jar"]

