├── kubelet
│
├── kube-proxy
│
├── Container Runtime
│
└── Pods
23. kubelet

Think:

kubelet = agent running on every worker node

It communicates with the Kubernetes API and makes sure Pods assigned to its node are actually running.

             Control Plane
                  │
                  │
            API Server
                  │
                  ▼
               kubelet
                  │
        ┌─────────┴─────────┐
        ▼                   ▼
 Container Runtime       Pod
24. kubelet responsibilities

Kubelet:

receives Pod specifications
creates/starts containers through the container runtime
monitors containers
reports node/pod status
executes probes
mounts volumes
handles lifecycle events
25. kubelet does not directly create containers

This is an important interview detail.

Modern Kubernetes uses the Container Runtime Interface (CRI).

Conceptually:

kubelet
   │
   │ CRI
   ▼
Container Runtime
   │
   ├── containerd
   └── CRI-O

Historically Docker was commonly used, but Kubernetes removed the built-in Docker runtime integration (dockershim) starting with Kubernetes 1.24.

So don't say:

kubelet talks directly to Docker.

Say:

kubelet communicates with a CRI-compatible container runtime such as containerd or CRI-O.

26. Container runtime

The runtime actually manages containers.

Examples:

containerd
CRI-O

Responsibilities include:

pull images
create containers
start/stop containers
manage container lifecycle
manage container filesystem
interact with Linux namespaces/cgroups
27. Image pulling

Suppose your Pod specifies:

image: myacr.azurecr.io/influencer-service:8f42a91

Flow:

kubelet
   │
   ▼
Container Runtime
   │
   ▼
Container Registry
   │
   ▼
Docker Image
   │
   ▼
Local Node

Then the runtime creates the container.

28. kube-proxy

This is related to Kubernetes networking.

Think:

kube-proxy helps implement Service networking on nodes.

Suppose you have:

Service
influencer-service
10.96.20.10:8080

and Pods:

Pod A → 10.1.1.5
Pod B → 10.1.2.7
Pod C → 10.1.3.8

The Service provides a stable virtual endpoint:

Client
   │
   ▼
Service
10.96.20.10:8080
   │
   ├── Pod A
   ├── Pod B
   └── Pod C

kube-proxy helps implement the traffic forwarding/load-balancing rules for Services.

Modern clusters may use iptables or IPVS, and some networking implementations replace or augment kube-proxy behavior.

29. Pods

Now we reach the most important workload concept.

Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.

Not container.

Not Deployment.

Pod.

30. Pod architecture

A Pod can contain one or more containers.

Pod
│
├── Container 1
│
├── Container 2
│
└── Shared:
     ├── Network namespace
     ├── IP address
     ├── localhost
     └── volumes

Example:

Pod
│
├── Spring Boot container
│
└── Sidecar container

Both share the Pod's network namespace.

31. Why multiple containers in a Pod?

Usually when containers are tightly coupled.

For example:

Pod
│
├── Spring Boot
│
└── Logging sidecar

or:

Pod
│
├── Application
│
└── Proxy

The containers share:

localhost
network
volumes
Pod lifecycle
32. Pod IP

Suppose:

Pod A
IP = 10.1.1.5

Containers inside that Pod can communicate using:

localhost

For example:

Spring Boot
localhost:8080

Sidecar
localhost:9000

They don't normally get separate Pod IPs.

33. Pod lifecycle

A simplified lifecycle:

Pending
   ↓
Running
   ↓
Succeeded

or:

Pending
   ↓
Running
   ↓
Failed

Containers themselves can restart within a Pod depending on restart policy/container state.

34. Kubernetes Service

Pods are ephemeral.

Suppose:

Pod A = 10.1.1.5

gets deleted.

New Pod:

Pod A = 10.1.4.9

Therefore clients shouldn't depend on Pod IPs.

That's why Services exist.

             Service
          10.96.20.10
               │
       ┌───────┼────────┐
       ▼       ▼        ▼
     Pod A   Pod B    Pod C

Service gives a stable endpoint.

35. Ingress

Ingress handles HTTP/HTTPS routing into the cluster.

Example:

Internet
   │
   ▼
Ingress
   │
   ├── /users     → user-service
   ├── /orders    → order-service
   └── /payment   → payment-service

But remember:

An Ingress resource itself doesn't necessarily implement the traffic routing. An Ingress Controller does.

Examples include NGINX Ingress Controller and cloud-provider controllers.

