53. Control Plane communication model

A useful mental model:

                 API SERVER
               /     |      \
              /      |       \
             ▼       ▼        ▼
        Scheduler  Controllers  kubelets
             │         │          │
             │         │          │
             └─────────┴──────────┘
                       │
                      etcd

More precisely, Kubernetes components communicate through the API server rather than arbitrary direct communication.

54. Kubernetes networking model

One of Kubernetes' core networking assumptions is:

Every Pod gets its own IP address, and Pods can communicate with other Pods without necessarily requiring NAT between them.

Conceptually:

Pod A
10.1.1.5
   │
   │
   ├──────────────► Pod B
   │               10.1.2.8
   │
   └──────────────► Pod C
                   10.1.3.9

The actual implementation comes from the cluster's CNI plugin.

Examples:

Calico
Cilium
AWS VPC CNI
Azure CNI
55. CNI

This is another component worth knowing.

CNI = Container Network Interface.

It is responsible for configuring networking for Pods.

When a Pod starts:

kubelet
   ↓
container runtime
   ↓
CNI
   ↓
Pod network
   ↓
Pod gets IP

So don't confuse:

kube-proxy ≠ CNI

CNI provides/configures Pod networking.

kube-proxy traditionally handles Service traffic rules.

56. CSI

Another interview term:

CSI = Container Storage Interface

It allows Kubernetes to interact with storage systems.

For example:

Pod
 ↓
PersistentVolumeClaim
 ↓
PersistentVolume
 ↓
CSI driver
 ↓
AWS EBS / Azure Disk / other storage
